  {
    "insert": "\nThe project aims to show what requirements LLM-based teaching agents need to fulfill \"[...] to personalize learning paths effectively for multiple subjects with different complexities\" and what ethical considerations could arise with an AI in teaching and tutoring. For that, the paper outlines an experiment, in which a group of undergraduate students will participate in tutoring sessions with an LLM-based AI tutor and a traditional human tutor.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "insert": "Strengths:",
    "attributes": {
      "bold": true
    }
  },
  {
    "insert": "\n"
  },
  {
    "insert": " ",
    "attributes": {
      "bold": true
    }
  },
  {
    "insert": "A teaser is important to clarify how the gaps found gaps from previous research will be addressed in your paper. Your work outlines, very clearly from the beginning, that you want to use LLM-Models to provide high-quality personalized lessons for every subject. You could improve the position of your research questions by linking them to other relevant work, which you can introduce on the \"anker\" part of your paper.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "insert": "Weaknesses:",
    "attributes": {
      "bold": true
    }
  },
  {
    "insert": "\nCorrect citation is a very important part of writing an academic paper, as it enables readers to verify the claims. Even though you linked 4 papers in your bibliography, you never used any of them. \"Researchers have recently found that students in a student-centered classroom are more engaged and achieve more than students in a traditional one-size-fits-all classroom.\", \"[...] but we didn’t have the human resources as well as the financial resources to achieve this goal.\", \"[...] researchers have come up with the idea of an AI tutor that can adjust to be suitable for each unique student in each different subject.\", \"LLMs like GPT-4 or LLama-2 have been excellent at generating human-like responses for tutoring, performing well in both accuracy and user satisfaction.\" and \"Sweller’s Cognitive Load Theory suggests that instructional control should keep the optimal level of complexity [...]\" are only the biggest examples in that regard. Moreover, \"Until now, there are a few services that provide education based on AI, but these are either too simplistic or too reactive.\" doesn't need a direct source but should have some kind of example. Please work on your citation. A tool like \"Zotero\" might help, as it can be exported directly into BibTeX.\n\nThe research questions outline what the paper should find out and should be as precise as possible. In your first research question, you outline that you want to work out the requirements for teaching agents. This question is too broad and does not define what kind of requirements you are working on, e.g., technical and infrastructural requirements are as included as algorithmic and programming requirements for efficient algorithms. Moreover, you plan on defining ethical considerations in your second research question. This question does not align with RQ1 and would broaden the paper to an extreme size. You should try to narrow down your questions and splitting them down into easier sub-questions.\n\nThe methodology should outline how you want to solve your research questions. Your experiment relies on one group of undergraduate students that \"[...] will participate in two tutoring sessions with a LLM-based AI tutor and a traditional human tutor in multiple subjects.\" Without any clarification, the reader can't understand whether the students learned the same concepts or even subjects from both tutors. Moreover, there is no information regarding any type of control group, and there is no context given on how you want to develop the LLM. When answering RQ1, what requirements are needed for a teaching agent, you base it on data collected comparing the quality between the human and the LLM-based AI tutor, without any specification of how requirements can arise from quality comparison. To answer RQ2, what ethical considerations should be made? You want to interview students and teachers regarding the user experience and effectiveness. Without any clear explanation, what students will be selected and where the teachers interact with the LLM in the first place. Try reworking the experiment with more depth in mind, and specifying in greater detail what you need, where you get it, and what data you expect to receive. Including this, review your schedule and set enough time aside to build the LLM.\n\nThe state of the art positions the work against existing research and specifies the gaps mentioned in \"introduction\" in greater detail. Your work doesn't seem to have a clear state of the art. The mention of Sweller's Cognitive Load Theory seems to refer more to a general problem with AI, linking it only remotely to LLM-based tutors. Try finding work that already introduced an LLM tutor and find a gap in their research to build up from that.\n\n"
  },
  {
    "insert": "Other:",
    "attributes": {
      "bold": true
    }
  },
  {
    "insert": "\n It seems that you wrote your bibliography by hand, not using BibTeX, since BibTeX hides sources when not cited. When using BibTeX, you can use the \"cite\" command, shown in the Template on page 4. This can simplify the citing inside your text. Furthermore, it seems that you're close to copying the schedule from the example.\n\n"
  },
